{"term":"popularity","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:cd2ee0e1617fdaece8","definition":"The quality or state of being popular; especially, the state of being widely liked or in favor with the people at large.","example":"This destination has increased in popularity after great reviews in the guide books.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"usually","register":"uncountable, usually","wordFamily":["nonpopularity","overpopularity","popularity contest","unpopularity"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/popularity#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:2ff40e5a6168ae4f33","definition":"The quality or state of being adapted or pleasing to common, poor, or vulgar people","example":"So this Gallant, labouring to avoid Popularity, falls into a habit of Affectation, Ten thousand times hatefuller than the former.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"archaic, usually","register":"archaic, uncountable, usually","wordFamily":["nonpopularity","overpopularity","popularity contest","unpopularity"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/popularity#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:e8ed68d0ef3c7ee0d1","definition":"Something which obtains, or is intended to obtain, the favor of the vulgar; claptrap.","example":"Popularities, and circumstances which […] sway the ordinary judgment.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"archaic, usually","register":"archaic, uncountable, usually","wordFamily":["nonpopularity","overpopularity","popularity contest","unpopularity"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/popularity#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:77d0decef4749308ab","definition":"The act of courting the favour of the people.","example":"Cato (the younger) charged Muraena, and indicted him in open court for popularity and ambition.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"obsolete, usually","register":"obsolete, uncountable, usually","wordFamily":["nonpopularity","overpopularity","popularity contest","unpopularity"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/popularity#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:778b4e87dbf7c97e84","definition":"Public sentiment; general passion.","example":"A little time be allowed for the madness of popularity to cease.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"archaic, usually","register":"archaic, uncountable, usually","wordFamily":["nonpopularity","overpopularity","popularity contest","unpopularity"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/popularity#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"}],"pronunciation":{"ipa":"/ˌpɒp.jəˈlæɹ.ɪ.ti/","us":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/e/e4/En-us-popularity.ogg/En-us-popularity.ogg.mp3"},"wordFamily":["nonpopularity","overpopularity","popularity contest","unpopularity"]}